New v5 assertions within the existing independent methods:
- a reviewed 11-minute lease is accepted by Native authorize at minute 9 while
  it is still unexpired, permitting an already armed callback to run;
- the same lease at minute 9 is rejected by default/full-window validation, so
  it cannot arm a new 10.5-minute session;
- the lease is rejected by Native authorize at its exact expiry boundary;
- changed source, stale expected worker session, and changed expected approval
  are each rejected before the fake backend can leave stopped state.
- a disposable FileStore remains pinned to the authenticated session directory
  after a synthetic active-pointer replacement, and its worker-idle barrier
  refuses while a phase lock is held then passes after release.

Review finding fixed during v5:
CLI originally checked session approval/session identity before the Engine
reloaded active state, leaving a retire-and-rearm TOCTOU. Engine._plan now checks
the expected session and expected approval on the exact immutable plan it
returns. FileStore binds that generation after rechecking active.json, so later
operations in the callback stay on the authenticated session directory. The
worker passes its argv session and canonical permit approval hash; start,
verify, and observe pass the permit approval hash. Cleanup/retire intentionally
use their separately reviewed stopped-state authority and worker-idle fences.

Conclusion:
PASS_OFFLINE_HOST_ENFORCEMENT_ENGINEERING_ONLY. The lifetime split prevents a
nearly expired lease from creating a new arm without preventing an unexpired,
exact-grant callback for an existing arm. It does not extend the approval,
accept expiry, guarantee scheduling before expiry, or establish live timer/host
behavior. Stage2/Stage3 qualification and new bootstrap authorization remain
required.
